  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 141 PM EDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\n- Slight Chances for Rain Saturday\n\nA negatively tilted ridge will continue to build as it extends\nfrom the southeast US across the Great Plains and the upper\nmidwest into Southern Canada. This is further amplified with high\npressure through the mid levels to the sfc. \n While the upper level ridge will maintain, the mid level and sfc\nhigh pressure will erode as a short wave perturbation creeps\nthrough the upper level ridge, bringing warm air advection from\nthe southwest tomorrow. \n That trough will have some mid level moisture that has been the\nreason for some consternation for tomorrow given any precipitation\nchances. \n As that moisture creeps into southern Lower from the southwest\ntomorrow, there could be enough mid level instability to allow for\nsome isolated showers tomorrow. \n While overall the precipitation chances continues to dwindle, the\nmid to near range guidance is showing slightly better chances,\naround 15 to 30 percent, and that would be early Saturday morning\nand late Saturday evening, after 8 PM. \n Any rainfall amounts will be light, with less than a tenth of\nprecipitation expected. \n \n- Warmer Than normal Trends trough the week\n\nWhile we fall behind in time this weekend, and we enter into\nNovember, the warm seasonal temperatures continue.\n An large upper level low will pass through the intermountain west\nand deepen as it moves through the Great Plains. The flow out\nahead of this system will keep the warm conveyor belt continuing \nthrough the week. While any precip that we discuss below will \nbring some cooling effects, overall temperatures will remain \naround 10 to 15 degrees above normal. Max temperatures will warm \nthrough the weekend, and will be in the mid to upper 60s through \nthe week. Lows temperatures overall are trending 10 to 20 degrees\nabove normal. Lows along and north of the US 10 corridor will be \nat or below freezing tonight. Otherwise, Low temps will rebound to\nabove freezing and remain there through most of the week. \n\n- Unsettled weather ahead with several precip chances next week\n\nThe above mentioned upper level low will be driving the weather\npattern over the next week. That low will bring several chances\nfor precipitation, the first being with a warm frontal band out\nahead of the low. There is a weak wave aloft, along with some 500\nmb vort maxes that correspond to a mid level negatively tilted\nshort wave that will pass through the Great Lakes region late \nMonday into Tuesday. While flow is divergent, NAEFS has PWATS\nadvecting through the Upper Peninsula, northern Lower and the\nnorthern portion of southern Michigan. Anomalous PWATS of around \n1.25 inches will linger over the state Tuesday. Both the GFS, EC \nand various ensembles show this trend. Given the current \nlocations, this should bring showers along and northward of the I \n96 corridor. This could envelop the rest of the state if the \nmoisture shifts slightly to the south. While the models begin to \ndiverge in a consensus, there remains a trend for the open wave \nto have a cold front pass through the area Wednesday into Thursday\nwith strong flow behind hit. There isn't much CAPE associated \nwith this system at this time and 850 mb temps behind the from \nrange from +6 to +9 C so not expecting snow with little chance for\nthunder.",
